{:edb/db-before-t 2649 :edb/db-after-t 2650 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535962 50 #inst "2026-10-09T11:45:05.862Z" 13194139535962 true] [17592186151956 1100 "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6$?" 13194139535962 false] [17592186151956 1100 "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6?$" 13194139535962 true] [17592186151956 1145 "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1\n$$" 13194139535962 false] [17592186151956 1145 "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1.\n$$" 13194139535962 true] [17592186151957 1100 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9$?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n2. $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n3. $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139535962 false] [17592186151957 1100 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9?$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139535962 true] [17592186151957 1145 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535962 true] [17592186151957 1145 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n1. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n$. Therefore, $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n2. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1$. Therefore, $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n3. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2$. Therefore, $\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535962 false] [17592186151958 1100 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" 13194139535962 true] [17592186151958 1100 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n2. $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n3. $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" 13194139535962 false] [17592186151958 1145 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n$\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100$\nand\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000$\nare both finite numbers.\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n$\\big\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N$.\nIndeed, we have that\n$\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0$\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n$. Therefore, for any $n\\in \\mathbb N$, there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n$, which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535962 false] [17592186151958 1145 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in \\mathbb N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535962 true] [17592186151959 1100 "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$?" 13194139535962 false] [17592186151959 1100 "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}?$" 13194139535962 true] [17592186151959 1145 "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1$.\n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 2= 6\n$$\n\nelements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$.\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m\n$$" 13194139535962 false] [17592186151959 1145 "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains $3\\cdot 2= 6$ elements, and is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/assets/f2ab6a78388c9be5a4b46d63916c8e299cb95746547189ed9fe2783938c007dc.png)\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m.\n$$" 13194139535962 true] [17592186152144 1020 #edb/ref 17592186376811 13194139535962 true] [17592186152144 1020 #e